1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,808
The school assigned these readings because a patient is more than a
medication calculation. You need to understand what was ordered, what
the patient actually took, how breathing can fail and whether your
proposed care is lawful and within your role.

2
00:00:14,808 --> 00:00:29,448
The paired reading below follows the assigned page spans in the
available candidate books. It teaches the narrative domains and
identifies incorrect or jurisdiction-specific statements rather than
reproducing every textbook sentence.

3
00:00:29,448 --> 00:00:42,792
This lecture links those domains to the Week Two airway and medication
stations. The practical airway sequence remains in its own lesson so
that a mental-health discussion never delays an effective breath.

4
00:00:42,792 --> 00:00:56,016
Use the source notes for unresolved edition and interactive material.
A detailed reading guide is useful learning content, but it is not
evidence that an unrecovered school activity has been completed.

5
00:00:56,016 --> 00:01:10,512
Begin with the patient and the reason for treatment. An amount can be
mathematically correct while the indication is wrong or a
contraindication makes it unsafe. Explain what improvement you expect
and what harm you are looking for.

6
00:01:10,512 --> 00:01:25,896
Verification includes the real package, formulation, expiry and
integrity as well as the order. Similar brand names, combination
products and a changed tablet strength can create an error before
anyone touches a syringe.

7
00:01:25,896 --> 00:01:40,944
Delivery means the medicine actually reaches the appropriate place at
the appropriate rate. A leaking IV, an uncoordinated inhaler or
swallowed sublingual tablet can change delivery even when the prepared
amount was correct.

8
00:01:40,944 --> 00:01:54,480
Finish with the patient again. Record what happened, including partial
or failed delivery, and reassess the effect. Reciting a list of rights
is preparation for that process, not a substitute for carrying it out.

9
00:01:54,480 --> 00:02:10,008
A usable order identifies the person, drug, amount, route and timing,
with relevant concentration, weight, limits and indication. A missing
route is not repaired by choosing whichever route seems fastest.

10
00:02:10,008 --> 00:02:24,816
An as-needed order still has a reason and safe boundaries. A stat
order changes urgency, not the obligation to check. Titration requires
a defined target, increments and reassessment under actual authority.

11
00:02:24,816 --> 00:02:38,088
Do not silently translate twice daily into every twelve hours or three
times daily into every eight hours. Those may be different schedules;
use the actual instruction and resolve uncertainty.

12
00:02:38,088 --> 00:02:50,376
For a verbal order, read back the complete medicine, units, route and
timing and obtain confirmation according to policy. Guessing is not
made safer by saying the scene was busy.

13
00:02:50,376 --> 00:03:05,880
The actual formulation determines how the medicine is prepared and
absorbed. Crushing an enteric-coated or modified-release product can
change its intended function; swallowing trouble needs an appropriate
plan, not automatic crushing.

14
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:21,024
A sublingual or buccal product uses mucosal delivery. A swallowed dose
is not necessarily equivalent. Rectal, vaginal, topical and
transdermal routes also need their own product, consent and scope
checks.

15
00:03:21,024 --> 00:03:35,760
For nasal medication, follow the actual device. Do not prime a sealed
emergency naloxone or glucagon spray by wasting part of its dose. The
dead-space arithmetic for a syringe atomizer is a separate process.

16
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:50,976
IV medicine enters the circulation without an absorption step, but
target effects can still take time. Check patency, compatibility, rate
and residual tubing volume rather than assuming a pushed syringe
proves successful treatment.

17
00:03:50,976 --> 00:04:05,808
A pressurized metered-dose inhaler uses propellant. A nebulizer may be
driven by compressed gas or electricity depending on the system. Do
not repeat the candidate text's implication that every inhaler needs
electrical power.

18
00:04:05,808 --> 00:04:20,664
Select and coordinate the actual spacer or mask with the person's
breathing. A severely distressed patient may be unable to perform an
ideal prolonged breath-hold, and a poorly sealed interface can waste
the prepared dose.

19
00:04:20,664 --> 00:04:34,368
Count what was actually administered, then assess whether the person
is moving air. A fading wheeze with reduced air entry and exhaustion
is not reassuring merely because the device ran for the expected time.

20
00:04:34,368 --> 00:04:46,416
If effective breaths are failing, provide ventilatory support and
reassess the pathway. Do not continue counting bronchodilator puffs
while ignoring a patient who needs assisted ventilation.

21
00:04:46,416 --> 00:05:00,312
Reassessment begins during administration when immediate harm is
possible. A textbook five-to-fifteen-minute interval is not permission
to wait through apnea, anaphylaxis or a suddenly painful swollen IV
site.

22
00:05:00,312 --> 00:05:15,360
Record the product, actual amount, route, relevant site, time, reason,
response and complication. If half the prepared medicine leaked or
delivery stopped, do not chart the whole planned dose as administered.

23
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:28,080
An incident report and a patient care record have different purposes.
Complete the required process without falsifying, deleting an
important finding or pre-charting treatment that has not happened.

24
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:42,768
After an error, first protect and assess the patient and get
appropriate assistance. Honest communication and system learning
follow; hiding an error prevents both effective rescue and prevention
of the next one.

25
00:05:42,768 --> 00:05:57,264
Explain the proposed care, important benefits and risks, alternatives
and the consequences of declining. Check understanding, not just
whether a form has a signature. A patient may understand and still
make a choice you dislike.

26
00:05:57,264 --> 00:06:10,896
Language difference, deafness, a psychiatric diagnosis or an unusual
appearance does not alone prove incapacity. Use appropriate
communication support and assess the actual decision the person is
making.

27
00:06:10,896 --> 00:06:24,456
Urgency does not erase a capable refusal. If the person cannot
participate, establish the actual emergency and legal pathway rather
than using implied consent as an unlimited permission label.

28
00:06:24,456 --> 00:06:39,672
After oxygenation, ventilation, glucose or poisoning improves, revisit
capacity and the explanation. Naloxone response alone does not
establish an informed refusal or remove the need for the current
refusal process.

29
00:06:39,672 --> 00:06:56,160
The assigned legal reading uses U.S. Nurse Practice Acts, state
boards, HIPAA and other U.S. structures. Learn the distinctions
between law, regulation, licence and policy without copying those
structures as BC paramedic legislation.

30
00:06:56,160 --> 00:07:10,296
A course certificate, an employer instruction and a textbook procedure
do not independently create every practice permission. Check the
actual licence, required education, service conditions and current
procedure.

31
00:07:10,296 --> 00:07:27,240
Likewise, neither a diagnosis nor a difficult interaction grants a
blanket power to restrain or transport someone involuntarily. Use
actual legal authority and the least restrictive appropriate approach,
with reassessment and consultation.

32
00:07:27,240 --> 00:07:43,296
Keep evidence factual: observations, timing, what was said, options,
reasoning, consultations and actual care. An adverse outcome alone
does not prove negligence, and a confident note cannot repair an
unsafe action.

33
00:07:43,296 --> 00:07:54,360
Ask for the exact product and formulation rather than accepting only a
familiar brand name. Inspect the package when available and compare
the usual strength with the recent refill.

34
00:07:54,360 --> 00:08:06,288
Explore timing, missed doses and extra doses without blame. A person
may be trying to follow instructions but misunderstand a changed
tablet strength or dosing device.

35
00:08:06,288 --> 00:08:19,416
Ask about alcohol, supplements, pain and cold medicines and other
substances. A prescribed sedative combined with an opioid may explain
respiratory depression better than either medication name alone.

36
00:08:19,416 --> 00:08:32,112
Do not force every finding into the psychiatric history. Hypoglycemia,
infection, trauma and medication toxicity remain possible in a person
with a mental-health diagnosis.

37
00:08:32,112 --> 00:08:45,552
Antipsychotic medications differ in their dopamine and serotonin
effects. Some movement reactions follow treatment and can be mistaken
for psychiatric deterioration if the exposure timeline is ignored.

38
00:08:45,552 --> 00:08:59,976
Acute dystonia involves sustained abnormal contractions. Neck, tongue
or airway involvement requires urgent assessment. Do not merely tell
the person to calm down or assume every unusual posture is deliberate.

39
00:08:59,976 --> 00:09:14,424
Akathisia can cause intense inner restlessness, while drug-induced
parkinsonism produces a different pattern of slowness and rigidity.
Tardive dyskinesia is another pattern, not an interchangeable name for
all of them.

40
00:09:14,424 --> 00:09:32,088
Ask about new treatment, dose changes and symptom onset; assess ABCs
and distress and communicate the suspected medication relationship.
Specific reversal treatment requires the appropriate authorized
pathway, not a guessed extra drug.

41
00:09:32,088 --> 00:09:47,976
Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is an emergency concern with relevant
exposure and a combination of altered awareness, marked rigidity,
temperature elevation and autonomic instability. It is not simply a
mild extrapyramidal effect.

42
00:09:47,976 --> 00:10:03,816
Serotonergic toxicity has a different medication context and important
neuromuscular findings such as clonus or hyperreflexia. Real
presentations can overlap; do not diagnose either from one temperature
or movement alone.

43
00:10:03,816 --> 00:10:18,456
An initially alert overdose patient can still deteriorate. Tricyclic
medicines can produce serious cardiac and neurologic toxicity, and
modified-release bupropion can produce delayed seizures and serious
effects.

44
00:10:18,456 --> 00:10:33,144
Preserve airway and breathing support, obtain the appropriate
assistance and convey the exposure, amount uncertainty, formulation
and time course. Do not substitute a psychiatric label for an acute
poisoning assessment.

45
00:10:33,144 --> 00:10:45,960
Lithium has a narrow safety margin and important renal handling.
Intercurrent illness, fluid and sodium changes and interacting
medicines can change risk even when the prescription is not new.

46
00:10:45,960 --> 00:11:03,360
Vomiting, diarrhea, tremor, poor coordination, slurred speech and
confusion deserve attention in the right context. Do not dismiss the
combination as a harmless side effect or wait for an exact laboratory
number before recognizing possible toxicity.

47
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:16,560
For a changed refill, calculate from the actual old and new labels
rather than tablet count alone. Carry the packaging or reliable
medication list into the handover and document the uncertainty
honestly.

48
00:11:16,560 --> 00:11:29,832
The role here is recognition, assessment and appropriate escalation.
This lesson does not prescribe a home dose change, fluid-and-salt
rescue plan or a psychiatric medication substitution.

49
00:11:29,832 --> 00:11:45,672
Benzodiazepines enhance inhibitory GABA-A function. Combined with
opioids, alcohol or other depressants, the concern includes impaired
airway protection and inadequate ventilation, not only whether the
person looks sleepy.

50
00:11:45,672 --> 00:11:59,136
Assess rate and effective depth, chest movement, air entry, awareness
and circulation. Supplemental oxygen can improve saturation while
carbon dioxide clearance remains inadequate.

51
00:11:59,136 --> 00:12:14,304
In suspected opioid poisoning, provide effective ventilation and use
naloxone under the actual pathway. An incomplete wake-up response can
reflect co-exposure; repeated medication is not a substitute for
giving a needed breath.

52
00:12:14,304 --> 00:12:26,136
The goal is adequate breathing with ongoing assessment. Do not
improvise a benzodiazepine antidote or conclude that a brief
improvement makes recurrence and other pathology impossible.

53
00:12:26,136 --> 00:12:39,072
Poisoning and withdrawal are not opposite labels that can be assigned
from agitation alone. Determine exposure and timing, assess physiology
and consider mixed substances and other illnesses.

54
00:12:39,072 --> 00:12:54,720
Opioid withdrawal commonly includes yawning, sweating, rhinorrhea,
pain and gastrointestinal symptoms. Significant illness, dehydration,
pregnancy or another substance withdrawal may change the risk and the
needed care.

55
00:12:54,720 --> 00:13:07,728
COWS is not an overdose meter and should not be repurposed as an
isolated stimulant-withdrawal scale. CIWA-Ar is an alcohol-withdrawal
assessment, not a universal benzodiazepine withdrawal tool.

56
00:13:07,728 --> 00:13:19,152
A score supports a defined assessment in an appropriate patient. It
never overrides an immediate airway threat or creates permission to
start an unauthorized medication regimen.

57
00:13:19,152 --> 00:13:34,512
Tolerance and physical dependence are not automatically opioid use
disorder. OUD is a clinical pattern involving impaired control and
significant harm or dysfunction, not a diagnosis made from housing
status or appearance.

58
00:13:34,512 --> 00:13:49,824
Naloxone reversal addresses an acute threat. Longer-term treatment can
include methadone, buprenorphine and other appropriate options.
Naltrexone is a different drug and purpose; do not confuse similar
names.

59
00:13:49,824 --> 00:14:04,464
BC treatment resources and their updates guide access and clinical
selection. Withdrawal management alone is not a prerequisite for
ongoing treatment, and psychosocial support should not be used as a
barrier to receiving medication.

60
00:14:04,464 --> 00:14:19,320
Discuss recurrence, loss of tolerance, the unpredictable supply,
naloxone access and safer-use support respectfully. Ask what the
person wants and provide a realistic connection instead of demanding
abstinence before offering help.

61
00:14:19,320 --> 00:14:33,408
The innovations reading compares therapies with very different
mechanisms and preparation. TMS is not ECT, and neither is a procedure
to reconstruct from a summary or use as a PCP treatment option.

62
00:14:33,408 --> 00:14:47,712
ECT requires individualized specialist screening, consent, anesthesia
planning and recovery assessment. Avoid universal fasting rules or
automatic exclusions based on one textbook sentence.

63
00:14:47,712 --> 00:15:02,064
For esketamine, the checked U.S. label requires at least two hours of
monitoring and a stability assessment before leaving. A per-device
amount is not the total regimen, and symptom improvement does not
prove prevention of suicide.

64
00:15:02,064 --> 00:15:15,792
Digital and remote care still need evidence, suitable access, privacy
and a practical emergency plan. A technology label does not remove
clinical risk or make a U.S. privacy declaration sufficient for BC.

65
00:15:15,792 --> 00:15:29,976
Follow air from nose or mouth through pharynx, larynx, trachea and
branching bronchi. Terminal bronchioles end the conducting zone;
respiratory bronchioles begin the region where gas exchange becomes
possible.

66
00:15:29,976 --> 00:15:44,808
Mucus, cilia and coordinated laryngeal protection reduce contamination
but do not make aspiration impossible. The epiglottis helps protect
the laryngeal entrance, not closes an opening from the esophagus.

67
00:15:44,808 --> 00:15:58,656
Type One alveolar cells provide much of the thin exchange surface.
Type Two cells produce surfactant at the inner air-liquid interface,
helping reduce surface tension; macrophages contribute to defense.

68
00:15:58,656 --> 00:16:12,120
The surface must also receive ventilation and perfusion. Naming an
alveolus is useful when you can explain why blocked airflow, fluid,
lost surface or poor flow can impair the whole exchange chain.

69
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:25,344
Diaphragm and chest movement create pressure changes. Compliance
describes volume change for pressure change; recoil is the tendency to
return. They are related but not the same property.

70
00:16:25,344 --> 00:16:38,760
Rapid shallow breaths may provide inadequate alveolar ventilation.
Hyperventilation means ventilation exceeds metabolic carbon dioxide
production, not simply a high respiratory rate.

71
00:16:38,760 --> 00:16:54,864
A normal saturation does not measure total oxygen content, tissue flow
or carbon dioxide removal. Likewise, a normal blood pressure does not
prove adequate microcirculation. Interpret the chain, not one
reassuring number.

72
00:16:54,864 --> 00:17:08,184
An exhausted person who becomes quiet and slow may be failing. Do not
use a paper bag or suppress breathing on an assumed anxiety diagnosis;
assess and support the actual physiological problem.

73
00:17:08,184 --> 00:17:25,728
An adult has taken an extra sedative and may also have used an opioid.
Breaths are shallow at five per minute and responses are brief. Oxygen
saturation is ninety-six percent after oxygen was applied. Pause and
decide whether this is reassuring.

74
00:17:25,728 --> 00:17:42,384
It is not reassuring. Open and assess the airway, clear obstruction or
secretions as needed and provide effective ventilatory support under
the trained pathway. Recheck chest movement, air entry and circulation
while obtaining the exposure history.

75
00:17:42,384 --> 00:17:58,656
If the opioid pathway applies, use its actual naloxone product and
reassessment plan without delaying a breath. Record the oxygen device,
dose actually delivered, time and response; do not invent a
benzodiazepine reversal order.

76
00:17:58,656 --> 00:18:12,048
As awareness improves, explain continuing risks and reassess decision-
making capacity. A briefly normal conversation does not by itself
establish a safe discharge or an informed refusal.

77
00:18:12,048 --> 00:18:54,336
Correct the classification before interpreting the patient. Table 4.2
places lamotrigine among second-generation antipsychotics, but it is
an antiseizure medicine also used for bipolar-I maintenance, not an
antipsychotic. Nor are lithium, valproate, carbamazepine and
lamotrigine one interchangeable voltage-channel-blocker mechanism. The
paragraph also confuses 5HT with 5-hydroxytryptophan/dopamine: 5HT
means serotonin, whereas 5-HTP is its precursor. A drug name, receptor
and indication must be checked separately rather than inherited from a
faulty table heading.

78
00:18:54,336 --> 00:19:32,136
Smoking history can change a medication story even when the tablet
dose is unchanged. Tobacco smoke can induce CYP1A2 and reduce
clozapine exposure; stopping that exposure can allow levels and
adverse effects to rise. The medication does not contain the metabolic
enzyme, as the textbook sentence implies. Ask about recent smoking
changes, admission to a smoke-free setting, new sedation, dizziness
and other symptoms, then communicate the change for clinician review.
Do not tell the patient to resume smoking or independently recalculate
the prescription.

79
00:19:32,136 --> 00:20:09,144
Use the source timing windows as history clues, not diagnostic
cutoffs. Dystonia often begins within hours to days after
starting/increasing an antipsychotic, akathisia and parkinsonian
features may emerge over the first weeks, and tardive movements often
follow months or years. Ask about recent injections, dose changes and
medicines removed as well as newly added. A distressed patient who
cannot sit still may be describing a drug effect; a rigid jaw or
tongue problem can threaten the airway and deserves immediate primary
assessment.

80
00:20:09,144 --> 00:20:44,808
Original six-step application: notice the medication change and new
jaw/voice abnormality; connect the timing and physical observations
without assuming a psychiatric cause; prioritize airway risk; choose
authorized support and early help; carry out and document those
actions; then evaluate the actual response and revise the plan. The
reading's clinic example waits an hour for a later documented review,
but that is not permission to postpone reassessment of an acute airway
threat. Use teach-back and the patient's concerns when discussing the
next steps.

81
00:20:44,808 --> 00:21:33,456
For the source injection comparison, intradermal delivery targets the
dermis at about 10-15 degrees; subcutaneous delivery targets fatty
tissue at 45-90 degrees; intramuscular delivery targets muscle at 90
degrees. Its example needle ranges are respectively 25-27 gauge and
one-quarter to one-half inch, 25-30 gauge and three-eighths to five-
eighths inch, and 20-25 gauge and five-eighths to one-and-a-half
inches. These are textbook examples, not a universal age/size
selection rule. Match tissue depth, medicine, device and the actual
approved procedure; neither a blanket pediatric length nor routine
aspiration for every IM product is justified.

82
00:21:33,456 --> 00:22:12,240
Read the three IM-site drawings by landmarks rather than by a general
body-region name. The deltoid site is located relative to the acromion
and surrounding structures; vastus lateralis uses the outer middle
third of the thigh, not the anterior rectus femoris; the ventrogluteal
site uses the greater trochanter, anterior superior iliac spine and
iliac crest. A buttock label is not permission to choose a
dorsogluteal site. Practise identifying the actual landmarks,
appropriate tissue and local exclusions with the instructor before
practising the authorized injection sequence.

83
00:22:12,240 --> 00:22:52,272
The assigned printed 676 already begins Advocacy, section 16.4. Its
opening objectives and narrative connect advocacy to speaking up for
patient needs and rights, helping the person obtain understandable
information and appropriate services, and supporting professional
standards. Advocacy means helping the patient voice their own
preferences, not replacing those preferences with the clinician's
choice. Patient-rights statements and nursing professional-rights
lists are important ethical/organizational tools but are not all
independently enacted BC statutes or authority to expand PCP scope.

84
00:22:52,272 --> 00:23:46,032
Keep adult-consent principles separate from the authority of a
particular practitioner. The BC adult consent Act defines a statutory
provider through prescribed legislation; Consent Regulation section 3
names the Health Professions and Occupations Act and Social Workers
Act, not the Emergency Health Services Act. Therefore do not assign an
EMA every hospital-provider or temporary-substitute-decision-maker
power simply by citing that Act. Use the actual EMA role, current
service consent/refusal pathway and consultation. Infants Act section
17 separately requires explanation, understanding of
nature/consequences and reasonably foreseeable benefits/risks, and
reasonable efforts to conclude the care is in the young person's best
interests; it is not a fixed birthday or a signature-only rule.

85
00:23:46,032 --> 00:24:33,264
BC safeguarding routes are not interchangeable. CFCSA section 14
requires prompt reporting when there is reason to believe a child
needs protection under section 13; the specified Indigenous-authority
route has its own confirmation requirement. Merely telling a
supervisor is not the statutory report. Adult Guardianship Act section
46 says a person may report to a designated agency where the section
44 circumstances apply; it is not a blanket mandatory report of every
adult concern, and capable adult choices remain relevant. Other
specific duties can still apply. Observe objectively, communicate
privately and use the actual applicable route rather than promise
secrecy or broadcast an allegation.

86
00:24:33,264 --> 00:25:11,328
The lawsuit diagram is a process map: a claim is filed and served, a
response is made, evidence is exchanged in discovery, and the matter
may settle or proceed to trial and judgment. The source example
includes a fourteen-day response step; that is not a BC deadline
taught by this course. A claim is not a finding of negligence, and a
settlement is not automatically an admission. When a complaint or
legal notice arrives, preserve records and follow the actual employer,
insurer and professional process rather than altering old notes or
contacting witnesses to align accounts.

87
00:25:11,328 --> 00:25:54,816
Trace the RAAS figure in order: the kidney releases renin, which acts
on liver-derived angiotensinogen to form angiotensin I; ACE helps form
angiotensin II. Pulmonary vascular endothelium is an important site,
not the only place ACE exists. Angiotensin II supports
vasoconstriction and adrenal aldosterone release; aldosterone promotes
sodium retention with water following. The diagram also shows ADH-
related water reabsorption. These mechanisms link lungs, kidneys,
vessels and volume regulation over time; they do not establish that
one BP reading proves adequate perfusion or authorize a drug or fluid
amount.

88
00:25:54,816 --> 00:26:36,960
The incentive spirometer picture illustrates a slow deep inspiration,
not blowing out through a peak-flow meter. With the prescribed device
and individual goal, the person inhales steadily, watches the
volume/feedback marker and demonstrates the technique back. Check
pain, understanding, cognition, hearing and the ability to use the
mouthpiece; a generic volume or schedule is not suitable for everyone.
Incentive spirometry supports a prescribed lung-expansion plan. It is
not assisted ventilation, CPAP or a diagnostic pulmonary-function
result, and must not delay help for an acutely failing patient.

89
00:26:36,960 --> 00:27:23,112
Use the arterial-gas table as a recognition exercise, not a treatment
order: the source pairs respiratory alkalosis with arterial pH above
7.45 and PaCO2 below 35 mmHg, and respiratory acidosis with pH below
7.35 and PaCO2 above 45 mmHg. Compensation and mixed disorders can
change the pattern, so interpret all available gases and the actual
patient. PaCO2 is arterial carbon dioxide pressure, not oxygen
saturation; PaO2 is arterial oxygen pressure, not the SpO2 display. A
fast respiratory rate does not by itself tell you any of these values.

90
00:27:23,112 --> 00:28:02,928
The assigned printed 792 already opens 19.2 Cardiovascular System. Its
introductory framework connects the pump, circulating volume and
vessels to tissue perfusion, then locates the heart in the mediastinum
and begins describing the great vessels. Use this opening to connect
breathing with circulation: oxygen reaching alveoli still needs blood
flow, hemoglobin and a functioning pump to reach tissues. The
assignment ends mid-description; it does not assign the entire
following cardiovascular section, and a normal BP alone cannot certify
the whole delivery system.

91
00:28:02,928 --> 00:28:42,120
The assigned chapter summary connects critical thinking to clinical
judgment. Use the six CJMM actions as a loop: recognize cues, analyze
them, prioritize hypotheses, generate solutions, take action and
evaluate outcomes. A hypothesis remains provisional. For example, a
person unable to finish a sentence needs an immediate breathing
assessment while the team gathers history; do not choose a familiar
diagnosis and stop noticing contradictory findings. Record relevant
positive and genuinely assessed negative findings, then explain how
the repeated assessment changes the plan.

92
00:28:42,120 --> 00:29:23,016
Check units before accepting a charted number. The source abdominal
chart pairs 89.3 degrees Fahrenheit with 38.5 degrees Celsius, but
they are not equivalent: 38.5 Celsius is 101.3 Fahrenheit, while 89.3
Fahrenheit is about 31.8 Celsius. The mismatch is a reason to verify
the original measurement, unit, time and patient, not to choose the
number that fits a preferred diagnosis. Apply the same discipline to
drug strength, laboratory units and times. This is a data-quality
lesson, not an answer to the source worked-case questions.

93
00:29:23,016 --> 00:29:59,928
Read the actual Figure 7.9 rather than its erroneous caption. Although
labelled a pain-treatment agreement, the displayed sheet explains U.S.
Prescription Drug Monitoring Programs: databases collect controlled-
substance prescription information, can reveal duplicate therapy or
interactions, and require appropriate privacy/access controls. A
monitoring database and a shared treatment agreement are different
tools. Neither its U.S. access rules nor its phone numbers become BC
instructions, and the presence of a prescription record does not prove
what the patient took today.

94
00:29:59,928 --> 00:30:43,896
Correct the SUD treatment comparison: methadone is an opioid agonist,
buprenorphine a partial agonist, and naltrexone an antagonist.
Naltrexone is not simply a weaker opioid used to wean a person off a
stronger one; starting it after recent opioid exposure can precipitate
severe withdrawal. Likewise, physical dependence is physiologic
adaptation with withdrawal risk after reduction, not by itself proof
of OUD or a moral failing. Ask what medicine is actually used and when
it was last taken; continuing treatment and acute pain both deserve an
individualized clinician plan. No initiation, stopping, taper or dose
is supplied here.

95
00:30:43,896 --> 00:31:23,808
Follow the auscultation figure by comparing matching levels side to
side, moving from upper to lower accessible fields on the front and
back when safe; avoid listening through bone or assuming a numbered
dot samples an entire lobe. Bronchial sounds are normally louder and
higher pitched centrally, bronchovesicular sounds intermediate over
larger bronchi, and vesicular sounds softer over peripheral fields.
Document site, phase, symmetry and intensity, not simply lungs clear.
A patient unable to tolerate repositioning needs adapted assessment
and immediate support, not completion of every dot first.

96
00:31:23,808 --> 00:32:09,552
Local pulmonary vascular responses can partly redirect flow away from
poorly ventilated regions, but this does not guarantee adequate total
gas exchange. Air reaching an alveolus without usable blood flow and
blood passing a poorly ventilated region are different mismatch
patterns. Connect the source table to separate mechanisms: narrowing
restricts airflow, reduced expansion/drive limits ventilation,
filling/collapse impairs exchange, low hemoglobin reduces carrying
capacity, and a vascular obstruction limits perfusion. Several can
coexist, so reassess both breathing and circulation instead of
assigning every abnormal saturation the same explanation.

97
00:32:09,552 --> 00:32:54,120
Read the concentration-time plot by its axes and thresholds. Time is
horizontal; plasma concentration is vertical. An illustrative curve
crosses an example minimum effective concentration, reaches a peak and
later falls below that threshold. The therapeutic window is the
concentration band between desired and adverse-response thresholds,
not the length of time between crossings and not a therapeutic-index
ratio. The source mixes Drug Effect with Cp and describes peak effect
as if it always equalled peak blood level; real effects can lag. Use
the actual person, exposure and response rather than predicting a safe
repeat dose or discharge time from this schematic.

98
00:32:54,120 --> 00:33:44,304
Use the actual label illustrations to separate content, concentration
and dose. The tablet label gives 125 mcg, also written 0.125 mg, per
tablet. The illustrated injection contains 100 mcg in 2 mL, equivalent
to 50 mcg/mL; the vial total is not the amount per millilitre. One
milligram equals one thousand micrograms. Generic name, strength,
route/form, expiry, single-dose designation and manufacturer serve
different checks. A controlled-substance symbol belongs to its
jurisdiction and does not establish BC scheduling or permission. These
are label-reading examples, not instructions to administer digoxin or
fentanyl.

99
00:33:44,304 --> 00:34:28,056
Ephedrine and epinephrine are not interchangeable names or products.
Both have adrenergic effects beyond airway smooth muscle, so pulse,
rhythm, blood pressure and interacting sympathomimetic/MAOI exposure
matter. The source ephedrine table calls intermittent asthma both an
indication and a contraindication; do not convert that contradiction
into a patient rule. Its epinephrine table also gives volumes without
a clear concentration in the sentence. Use the actual current
indication, product concentration, route and local protocol. An
inhaled product or oral ephedrine example is not a replacement for the
anaphylaxis IM pathway.

100
00:34:28,056 --> 00:35:15,360
The last assigned printed697already begins25.2and includes its
objectives, inflammation illustration and first drug table.
Beclomethasone is the inhaled corticosteroid example, while prednisone
is an oral systemic example; they are not interchangeable formulations
or an immediate bronchodilator. The figure contrasts airway wall
inflammation, mucus and narrowed lumen with an unobstructed example.
Corticosteroids address inflammation on the relevant treatment
timescale, not rescue ventilation. The full following steroid
discussion is outside this page span, and neither inhaled steroids for
every COPD patient nor the wide oral dose range is made a blanket
instruction here.

101
00:35:15,360 --> 00:36:01,656
Use the cardiovascular name stems as recognition prompts, then verify
the real medicine: -pril commonly suggests an ACE inhibitor, -sartan
an angiotensin-receptor blocker, -dipine a dihydropyridine calcium-
channel blocker, -lol a beta blocker, -osin an alpha blocker and
-dralazine the hydralazine vasodilator example. The source -ide
examples include furosemide and hydrochlorothiazide, but -ide is far
too broad to identify all diuretics. The -vastatin examples concern
lipid-lowering drugs, not an immediate blood-pressure treatment.
Knowing a class helps focus the exposure history; it supplies neither
an indication nor a dose for this patient.

102
00:36:01,656 --> 00:36:42,216
Test the naming rule against exceptions. Aripiprazole is not a proton-
pump inhibitor merely because its name ends in prazole. The source
separates -statin examples such as cilastatin/pepstatin from lipid-
lowering -vastatin examples. Its minaxolone entry refers to a steroid-
structured anesthetic studied historically, not permission to classify
it as an anti-inflammatory corticosteroid. A drug class may describe
chemistry, mechanism or therapeutic use, which are different
classification axes. Resolve the actual generic product and mechanism
before using a suffix to explain the patient.

103
00:36:42,216 --> 00:37:24,408
For nervous-system and symptom medicines, -azepam suggests the
benzodiazepine examples, -barb- the barbiturates, -caine local-
anesthetic examples, -axine the venlafaxine antidepressant example,
-peridol and -peridone antipsychotic examples, and -pidem a hypnotic
such as zolpidem. Do not assume every barbiturate is being used for
seizures or every antidepressant for the same diagnosis. Ask about
actual indication, recent changes, sedation, breathing and
interactions. A shared naming fragment does not make two products
interchangeable or prove that an observed behaviour is psychiatric.

104
00:37:24,408 --> 00:38:06,048
The anti-infective and other source stems include -cillin for
penicillin-family beta-lactams, -cycline for tetracycline examples,
-oxacin for fluoroquinolones, -conazole for antifungals and -vir for
antiviral examples. Other recognition groups are -atadine
antihistamines, -ac and -profen NSAID examples, -terol bronchodilators
and the omeprazole -prazole proton-pump-inhibitor example. These cues
help ask targeted questions about allergy, interactions and intended
use. They do not determine infection type, rescue-versus-maintenance
use or whether an unfamiliar same-ending medicine belongs to that
class.

105
00:38:06,048 --> 00:38:55,536
Original gravity-flow rehearsal: a simulation instruction specifies
180 mL over 60 minutes using a set labelled 20 gtt/mL. Calculate 180
mL times 20 gtt/mL divided by 60 minutes = 60 gtt/min. Reverse-check:
60 gtt/min times 60 minutes divided by 20 gtt/mL = 180 mL. A pump
would express this example as 180 mL/hour, not 60 mL/hour. A different
set factor changes the drop rate. This is unit practice, not a fluid
order; verify the actual authorized amount, patient, access, delivery
system and response.

106
00:38:55,536 --> 00:39:41,688
Original daily-dose rehearsal: an unnamed training medicine is ordered
at 8 mg/kg/day for an 18 kg simulation patient, divided every 12
hours, with a training concentration of 24 mg/mL. Daily amount is 144
mg/day; two administrations per day give 72 mg/dose; 72 mg divided by
24 mg/mL gives 3 mL/dose. Reverse-check both the dose and the daily
total. If the instruction were per kilogram per dose, this calculation
would be different. No real pediatric drug, safe maximum or permission
to administer has been supplied by this exercise.

107
00:39:41,688 --> 00:40:28,968
Compare the actual inhaler motions. A pressurized MDI coordinates a
slow inhalation with actuation, with an appropriate spacer/valved
chamber when indicated. A DPI needs adequate device-specific
inspiratory effort; load or pierce its capsule only as instructed,
exhale away, and never swallow an inhalation capsule. A soft-mist
inhaler has its own preparation and dose-release sequence with a slow
inhalation. Rinsing and spitting after an inhaled corticosteroid
addresses local exposure, not a universal rule that every inhaler
contains a steroid. Ask for return demonstration when stable; device
preparation must not delay support for ineffective breaths.

108
00:40:28,968 --> 00:41:07,488
The source sequence of bronchodilator, dornase alfa, airway clearance,
inhaled antibiotic and steroid is a specific multi-treatment context,
not an order to give every breathless person all five. Match each
prescribed treatment to its purpose and the person's actual care plan.
Bronchodilation, secretion modification, antimicrobial treatment and
anti-inflammatory control solve different problems. Check what was
already taken, whether the person can use the device and how the
response changes the next action; do not turn the list into an
automatic PCP treatment stack.

109
00:41:07,488 --> 00:41:46,800
Read the drain photographs by system and purpose. Hemovac and Jackson-
Pratt examples use a compressed reservoir to provide closed-system
suction; a Penrose example allows passive drainage into a dressing. A
pigtail describes a curled catheter shape, not by itself the
destination, fluid or suction plan. The source notes emptying closed
reservoirs around one-third to one-half full and measuring output, but
a hospital shift schedule is not an EMS monitoring interval. Know what
is connected, keep it secured and communicate the actual type, site
and output trend before moving the patient.

110
00:41:46,800 --> 00:42:33,792
Original integrated encounter: a postoperative adult has new drain
leakage, increasing sleepiness after an extra home analgesic and very
shallow breaths despite a reassuring saturation on oxygen. First
assess/support the immediate airway and breathing threat, obtain help
and reassess circulation; do not complete a dressing change before
restoring effective breaths. Then reconcile the actual medicine,
amount and timeline, describe the wound/device/output change and
preserve the appropriate system during transfer. Handover should
connect observations, actual actions and response, not diagnose every
symptom from the drain or dismiss deterioration because a monitor
number looks normal.

111
00:42:33,792 --> 00:43:26,472
Keep the source oxygen numbers with their setting. Checklist 42
mentions an expected saturation above 95%, a general treatment band of
92-98% and a low-start nasal-prong example. The historical flowchart
instead branches at 88% or below versus 89-91%, with different initial
nasal flows and five-minute reassessment. Table 5.6 discusses 88-92%
for a known chronic-retention context. These are not one internally
identical algorithm, a rule to wait five minutes during failure or a
reason to start every critically ill person on low-flow prongs. Use
the actual school/current BCEHS indication, device, target and
exception pathway; treat ineffective ventilation as a different
immediate problem.

112
00:43:26,472 --> 00:44:19,512
Distinguish the source oral Yankauer procedure from its flexible
oropharyngeal-catheter procedure: they use different equipment,
depth/asepsis considerations and monitoring. The actual 2018 copy also
prints the unresolved portable 10-15 mmHg value beside a 100-150 mmHg
wall-suction range; do not silently reinterpret the unit as kPa or
transplant either number to every device/age/route. Use the school-
trained method and verified device settings, clear the obstruction
without prolonged suction or tissue trauma, and reassess between
attempts. Worsening oxygenation, bleeding, distress or ineffective
breaths calls for stopping/reassessment and appropriate support, not
finishing a textbook sequence first.

113
00:44:19,512 --> 00:45:03,384
The scheduled-medication table distinguishes locally defined time-
critical medicines from other scheduled medicines: it gives a thirty-
minute window for selected time-critical orders, one hour for some
other repeated schedules and two hours for daily/weekly/monthly
examples. This is a hospital scheduling framework, not a universal
allowance to delay an emergency medicine or redefine PRN repeat
limits. Clarify the actual order and current policy, then record the
time actually administered. An electronic MAR checked by two people
can still contain the same stale order; reconciliation must compare
actual patient use and the authoritative current instruction.

114
00:45:03,384 --> 00:45:50,040
For a feeding-tube medicine, verify the tube type/destination, the
approved placement check and the actual product/formulation before
delivery. A whoosh, aspirate appearance or lack of distress alone does
not prove initial NG placement; the NHS enduring standard uses a
validated safe-range pH result or appropriate x-ray confirmation in
its setting. Give suitable medicines separately and follow the
individualized flush/feed plan. The book's 30/15/30 mL flush and feed-
interruption examples are not universal pediatric or fluid-restricted
instructions. Do not force resistance, mix everything into feed or use
an uncertain tube merely because the syringe fits.

115
00:45:50,040 --> 00:46:03,168
Without notes, explain why a correct milligram calculation can still
lead to unsafe care. Include the patient, indication, formulation,
route, delivery and response in your answer.

116
00:46:03,168 --> 00:46:15,072
Then distinguish opioid poisoning, opioid withdrawal and ongoing
opioid use disorder treatment. Name what COWS does, what it does not
do and why naloxone is not naltrexone.

117
00:46:15,072 --> 00:46:25,584
Explain two medication-related red flags that should not be dismissed
as psychiatric behaviour, and one respiratory finding that overrides a
normal saturation display.

118
00:46:25,584 --> 00:46:40,104
Finally open the book-specific bilingual guides and work through the
original retrieval prompts. Keep the exact school edition and
unrecovered activities visible in the coverage record rather than
marking them complete by familiarity.
